artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider conformal field theories (CFTs) in dimension $d\ge 2$ in the presence of boundaries and/or extended defects (submanifolds of positive codimension on which the bulk fields can have prescribed singular behavior). A boundary or defect is called conformal if it preserves the subgroup of the conformal group that fixes it. Associated to such conformal boundaries and defects one has: (i) renormalization-group (RG) domain walls, i.e. codimension-one conformal interfaces obtained by joining a UV CFT to its IR CFT under an RG flow; (ii) non-perturbative bCFT/dCFT data such as bulk-boundary OPE coefficients, defect operator spectra, one-point functions of bulk operators, and crossing-symmetric correlators; (iii) candidate monotonicity (or "$C$-/$F$-/$g$-/$b$-/$a$-type") theorems for boundaries and defects in dimensions $d\ge 3$, asserting the existence of a quantity attached to a conformal boundary/defect that decreases monotonically under boundary or defect RG flows; (iv) the problem of classifying all conformal boundary conditions and conformal defects in a given bulk CFT.

Develop a precise mathematical framework, drawing on the following toolkits, that addresses (i)–(iv):
\begin{enumerate}
\item Supersymmetric localization and superconformal representation theory for theories with extended supersymmetry, providing exact results for protected boundary/defect observables;
\item the conformal bootstrap, i.e. the program of constraining bCFT/dCFT correlators by crossing symmetry, unitarity, and the bulk and defect operator spectra;
\item integrability of bulk and boundary/defect theories, including exact $S$-matrices, reflection matrices, and integrable lattice realizations;
\item topological field theory methods, including the use of topological defects, fusion categories, module categories, and higher categorical structures encoding boundary conditions;
\item additional bCFT/dCFT techniques such as holography, anomaly inflow, and the analytic conformal bootstrap.
\end{enumerate}

Using these tools, formulate and prove:
\begin{itemize}
\item Existence and classification statements for RG domain walls between pairs of CFTs related by RG flow;
\item Rigorous determinations of non-perturbative boundary/defect CFT data in nontrivial examples;
\item Monotonicity theorems for a boundary/defect $C$-function in dimension $d\ge 3$, generalizing Affleck–Ludwig's $g$-theorem in $d=2$ and Casini–Huerta–Myers-type entropic monotonicity results;
\item A classification of conformal boundary conditions and conformal defects of arbitrary codimension in distinguished families of CFTs (e.g.\ free theories, minimal-like models, $\mathcal{N}\ge 2$ superconformal theories).
\end{itemize}

To what extent can each of these aims be achieved with currently available techniques, and what new structural results follow?

